A||||15||Spektyr||Swaps:  Cost vs. Ease vs. Power||||5||
Z||000000||Spektyr||08-28-2001||09:45 AM||||Yeah, this has been beat to death.  I just spent an hour or so reading as much as I could find and still I'm a bit curious.<P>I've got a sick 2.8 in my 85GT, I suspect it's barbecued a rod bearing.  While this motor may find it's way back into a Fiero, it probably won't happen soon, and won't happen to my car.  I've got bigger plans...<P>13 second kind of plans.<P>My primary engine considerations are the 350SBC and the 3800SC II.  My primary considerations are final cost, ease of swap (first time engine swapper here), and bang for the buck.  Minor considerations are economy, engine accessibility for repairs, and cost of repair parts.<P>Personally, I like the 3800SC's kick a$$ performance, economy, and the supercharger whine.  However, having read up on what all is involved in the swap it sounds like it may just be too much for me to accomplish in my own garage.  On the flip side (350SBC) it would be fun to say I've got a bigger motor in my Fiero than my Town Car...<P>I'm also considering scratching together the extra money I'd need to have the motor installed professionally.  While I'd lose the pride of DIY, I'd gain the likelyhood of it NOT breaking down shortly afterwards because I missed some small detail.  (like a dogbone)<P>My hope is to have at least $5,000 to spend on the project over the course of the next several months, and the less it costs the sooner I'm cruising in my Fiero again (and the sooner I can get the suspension, paint, interior, etc done).<P>So this is the question I put to you all.<BR>What is the best solution to my desire to build a car that does the quarter in under 14 sec, can be built by someone that knows his way around a motor but hasn't yanked one yet, will give me the best bang for the buck, and won't break my knuckles or pocketbook to maintain?  Also, am I leaving any motor options out?<P>(and don't be shy to mention if you've got one of those motors laying around)<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-28-2001||10:07 AM||||V8 Archie for the V8 swap..Cost depends on how good a deal you get for the engine you want.  He has a thread on the forum for keeping cost below $3000. But I'd expect more like $5000+ for a good engine plus other upgrades to set up handeling (i.e. suspension, brake upgrades).<P>Ed Parks (Alabama)or Bob Steiger (Illinois)do a good job at 3800S/C swaps. Probably best driving economy for gas milage as long as you are not drag racing from stop light to stop light. Costs using a new or low milage 3800 still probably in the $5K range.<P>I tend to estimate high, then I'm not disappointed when the cost over run my budget.  Everything seems to cost more than I'd like, but Archie, Ed or Bob are making a living at this kind of stuff and have seen the "problems" doing the swap installs before and know the fixes.<P>Quarter mile times ?? The potential is there, but much is up to the driver's ability.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ambush||08-28-2001||10:07 AM||||Don't let this thread die. And t add a little something. not trying for a 13 second car but I do want 200+ horses. Thinking of the firebird swap or a V8- which is cheaper as far as labor and kits needed. Engine wise I can get those pretty cheap. So should I go with the engine swap or would a turbo be sufficient?<p>[This message has been edited by Ambush (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||08-28-2001||12:13 PM||||i'd like to try it. possibly twice. the pre-northstar caddy is in the lead now for my '87 coupe. from what i've read it seems to be easiest from a techno and price point. it has that 200 horse potential (gross anyway. not sure about at the wheels). not sure about the other, but something that would get the most fun out of a 4:10 tranny. <p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||08-28-2001||01:38 PM||||After having done a 3.4L DOHC, 3800SC and basic Fiero upgrades, I think the 3.4L upgrade (not-DOHC) is the best cost vs performance. The 3800SC is the best performance vs effort (labor). I wouldn't consider a V8 other than the NS and that is not very good for cost vs performance.<P>IMHO.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||08-28-2001||04:58 PM||||Well, a 13-second quarter mile car is no mean feat...  this is faster than an Acura NSX for example.<P>The two swaps you mention will get you there, if done right.  To pay a pro, expect to pay $6-7k for a drive-away car by the time you find an engine, buy their kit, AND pay them to install it...<P>You can probally do it for half this cost in your garage, but you will need tools ($) and  time ($$$) and mostly knowledge ($$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$) ;)<P>If a dogbone (engine torque strut) is a 'little thing' then you should probally have a pro build it.  (it will also happen much quicker, you won't have to wait a year or so, like me :)<P>3.4 v6 you can do if you can change a 2.8, and the 4.9 caddy v8 is also a candidate, both are around 200hp, the v8 is far torquier though.  Neither are 13-sec 1/4 candidates however.  Both these engines are direct bolt-up to your tranny.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||lildevil||08-28-2001||07:28 PM||||I ve done the 3800SC swap and heres what i spent.<P>MOtor,trans,pcm,axles,and odds and ends cost me 2500 dollars and its from a 98 Regal with only 5000 miles.<P>Mounts from Chris West  ....    200 dollars<BR>Have CVs swapped.............   100<BR>Wire Harness done(Chris west)...300<BR>Did all the work myself.........priceless<P>So for under 3500 dollars you'd have a mid to high 13 second car that gets about 30 miles to the gallon Highway. Mine is a auto by the way. Spend another 100 dollars on a smaller pulley and you'll have a low 13 second car. Spend another hundred dollars on a even smaller pulley...12's?  Mods for the 3800 are becoming rather plentiful. Although it doens't have the sound or the prestige as the V-8 swaps, the 3800 SC is definitely a bang for your buck in my opinion.<P>------------------<BR>Earl Sessions<BR>1985 Fiero w/ 1998 Supercharged 3.8 V-6<BR>Best ET: 13.232 @ 101.24 mph<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/water3quarterthumb.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Spektyr||08-29-2001||12:02 PM||||Thanks for all the input on this.  To address a couple of the issues brought up here:<P>Knowledge: I know how motors work and most of the various little systems that are bolted onto the modern ones (EGR, etc).  I like old-fashioned motors... back when if it didn't make the motor run, it wasn't bolted on, but I'm an electronics tech, so high-tech doesn't scare me.<P>Tools: I've got all the basic stuff, and what I don't have I can rent from a local parts store.  What I can't rent I can buy or steal.  (Oops... didn't mean steal.)<P>Money: Doing some rather insane amounts of overtime (30-60hrs a month extra) I can pull in about $1k a month that I can dedicate to the car, leaving myself cash for bills, food, etc.<P>Time: I'm not the most patient person.  While I'm smart enough to not rush things at the expense of quality, I REALLY want this car running for next summer.<P>Since these cars seem to always cost us more money than we'd like, I'm wondering what tips/tricks other people on this board have discovered for getting the dough to spend on their Fieros.  I've been toying with the notion of getting a loan on my Town Car and/or Fiero to help get the cash for the project.  Opinions on this?  Good idea?  Bad?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 08-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||08-29-2001||02:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR><B>Since these cars seem to always cost us more money than we'd like... I've been toying with the notion of getting a loan on my Town Car and/or Fiero to help get the cash for the project.  Opinions on this?  Good idea?  Bad?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Think about it this way: <BR>Buy a cheap car (Fiero), get cheap thrills. When done with it, it's still a cheap car (i.e, It's hard to lose much money if you didn't pay too much to start).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Now, take that cheap car and dump $5000 into it. The thrills are better, your friends are envious, the 5.0 guys a furious. :P <P>You decide to borrow the money to make it fast. Payments are affordable. If you decide to sell the car, The worse that can happen is you take a few thousand less than you have into it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>But, the bright side is it'd still be cheaper than the value lost to depreciation of say... a mid 90's Camaro with an LT1 (or was that LS1?). And, you'd have the advantage of driving something truly unique.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||6
Z||000009||lowCG||08-29-2001||07:31 PM||||I agree with Terry,in that the 3.4 pushrod is a great choice,if your car's engine is in good shape otherwise;that is including all of the little things that are attached to the motor itself.<BR>After adding up what it would take to replace or fix all the old junk on my 2.8,I decided to put in a 3.4TDC,did it all within a month's time,while working 55+ hours a week,and drove it across the US a week after the install.<BR>Anything more than this and you'll need to really beef-up the subframe,and find a clutch that isn't made yet.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 08-29-2001).]||||reg||1
